    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein.jpg" alt="Karl I, Prince of Liechtenstein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Karl I, Prince of Liechtenstein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Karl_I_of_Liechtenstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deutschland Maler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principality of Liechtenstein takes its name not from its territory but from the dynasty that bought it. The Liechtenstein family, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein-castle">Liechtenstein Castle</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, had become one of the richest noble houses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, with vast estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Karl I</a> of Liechtenstein was raised to the rank of prince in 1608. To gain a seat and vote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-princes">Council of Princes</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, however, the family needed a territory held directly from the emperor (<em>reichsunmittelbar</em>). The opportunity came from the debts of the Counts of Hohenems, who were forced to sell their possessions on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-adam-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Johann Adam Andreas of Liechtenstein</a> bought the Lordship of Schellenberg in 1699 and the County of Vaduz in 1712. On 23 January 1719 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VI</a> united the two and raised them to an imperial principality named Liechtenstein for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-florian-prince-of-liechtenstein">Anton Florian</a>. The new state covered about 160 square kilometres between the Rhine valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and had only a few thousand inhabitants, mostly farmers. Before the purchase, Vaduz and Schellenberg had passed through the hands of a series of noble families, among them the counts of Werdenberg-Sargans, the lords of Brandis and the counts of Sulz, before the Hohenems.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/schaan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schaan.jpg" alt="Schaan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/schaan">Schaan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schaan,_die_Pfarrkirche_Sankt_Laurentius_in_straatzicht_foto7_2014-07-21_10.12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michielverbeek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history Liechtenstein was a poor agricultural country of small farms, vineyards and Alpine pastures. Many men worked seasonally abroad, often in the building trades, and emigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was common in the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> was abolished in 1808, but the population remained small, under 10,000 in 1900. Textile mills opened from the 1860s, and a customs treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> from 1852 tied the economy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> market until 1919. The economy was transformed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> with Switzerland of 1923. The Persons and Companies Act of 1926 attracted holding companies and foundations, and the country built a large financial sector. From the 1950s industry expanded rapidly: firms such as Hilti, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schaan">Schaan</a> in 1941, and Ivoclar Vivadent became international manufacturers of construction tools, dental products and precision equipment. Today Liechtenstein, with about 40,000 inhabitants, has one of the highest incomes per head in the world and roughly as many jobs as residents, with many workers commuting daily from Switzerland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The population is mostly Roman Catholic, the Catholic Church is the state church under the constitution, and German is the official language, while local Alemannic dialects are spoken in daily life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war.jpg" alt="Austro-Prussian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht-bei-koeniggraetz-von-georg-bleibtreu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Bleibtreu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liechtenstein’s military history is short. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> it contributed to the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a>, and as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> from 1806 it owed a small contingent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s armies. As a member of the German Confederation from 1815 it was required to keep a federal contingent, which numbered about 80 men by the 1860s. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 this force, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was sent to guard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stelvio-pass">Stelvio Pass</a> on the Tyrolean border against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; it saw no combat and, according to a well-known story, came home with one more man than it had set out with. After the German Confederation dissolved, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Johann II</a> disbanded the army in 1868, judging it pointless and too costly, and Liechtenstein has had no armed forces since. It remained neutral and unoccupied in both world wars. In 1945 it gave asylum to about 500 soldiers of the First Russian National Army, Russians who had fought on the German side under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-smyslovsky">Boris Smyslovsky</a>, and refused Soviet demands for their extradition; some later returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and others emigrated, many to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Security now rests on neutrality and close relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and a small national police force is the only armed body. The constitution still obliges men to defend the country in an emergency.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-i-joseph-prince-of-liechtenstein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-i-joseph-prince-of-liechtenstein.jpg" alt="Johann I Joseph, Prince of Liechtenstein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-i-joseph-prince-of-liechtenstein">Johann I Joseph, Prince of Liechtenstein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Josef_I_von_Liechtenstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Baptist von Lampi the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liechtenstein is a constitutional hereditary monarchy in which the reigning prince holds unusually strong powers. For its first two centuries it was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where the princes lived, through a governor (<em>Landvogt</em>) in Vaduz; no reigning prince made his home in the country until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Franz Joseph II</a> moved there in 1938. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-i-joseph-prince-of-liechtenstein">Johann I</a> issued a constitution based on the estates in 1818, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Johann II</a> granted a constitution in 1862 that created an elected Landtag. After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the constitution of 5 October 1921 defined the state as a constitutional hereditary monarchy on a democratic and parliamentary basis, with state power resting in both the prince and the people. The Landtag of 25 members is elected by proportional representation, the government is appointed by the prince on the Landtag’s proposal, and citizens have extensive rights of initiative and referendum on the Swiss model. Women gained the vote at national level only in 1984, after referendums in 1971 and 1973 had rejected it. In a 2003 referendum voters approved constitutional amendments proposed by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-adam-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Hans-Adam II</a>, which confirmed his power to veto laws and gave him the power to dismiss the government, while also allowing citizens to abolish the monarchy by referendum. Since 2004 his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alois-hereditary-prince-of-liechtenstein">Hereditary Prince Alois</a>, has exercised the day-to-day duties of head of state as his representative.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ginevra-de-benci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ginevra-de-benci.jpg" alt="Ginevra de' Benci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ginevra-de-benci">Ginevra de' Benci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Ginevra_de%27_Benci_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leonardo da Vinci, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liechtenstein’s significance lies less in power than in survival. It is often described as the only member of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-princes">Council of Princes</a> that survives as a sovereign state, and with Monaco, San Marino and Andorra it is one of the European microstates that outlived the consolidation of larger nations. Its route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, from imperial fief through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> to full independence after the German Confederation dissolved in 1866, shows how small territories survived by attaching themselves to larger patrons, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The princely family’s art collection, one of the most important private collections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, includes major works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a> and is shown in the family’s Garden Palace and City Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>; sales from it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when the family lost its estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, helped sustain the dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ginevra-de-benci">Ginevra de’ Benci</a></em> was sold to the National Gallery of Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington</a> in 1967. In the twentieth century Liechtenstein became a model of a microstate economy based on finance and specialised industry, but also a byword for tax secrecy, notably in the 2008 affair in which leaked bank data exposed German tax evaders. It has since adopted international standards of tax transparency and information exchange.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liechtenstein’s transformation into a modern state came through the loss of its old patrons. The dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806 left it nominally sovereign within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, and after 1815 it joined the German Confederation; when that body dissolved after the war of 1866, Liechtenstein was left fully independent, though still closely bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> through the customs treaty of 1852 and Austrian administration of its postal service. The fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy in 1918 brought economic hardship, as the Austrian krone collapsed, and turned the country towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>: it ended the Austrian customs treaty in 1919, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> treaty with Switzerland in 1923 and adopted the Swiss franc as its currency in 1924, and Switzerland has represented it diplomatically in many countries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Franz Joseph II</a>, who reigned from 1938 to 1989, was the first prince to live permanently in Vaduz; under him the country stayed neutral during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and grew prosperous. Liechtenstein joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> in 1978, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1990, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-free-trade-association">European Free Trade Association</a> as a full member in 1991 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-area">European Economic Area</a> in 1995, and it became part of the Schengen area in 2011. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-adam-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein">Hans-Adam II</a> succeeded his father in 1989. Today Liechtenstein is a wealthy, stable microstate of about 40,000 people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Liechtenstein, a principality created in 1719 so that a great Austrian noble family could sit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, outlived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the German Confederation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy to become a sovereign microstate that combines a powerful hereditary monarchy with direct democracy and one of the world’s highest incomes per head.</em></p>

